    <briefdescription>
<para>The complete monomial basis <formula id="164">$ \boldsymbol{H}(\boldsymbol{x}) $</formula> of order <formula id="162">$ n $</formula> in <formula id="160">$ d $</formula> dimensions, as the list of the factors of its entries. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The entries are all monomials <formula id="165">$ x_1^{\alpha_1} \cdots x_d^{\alpha_d} $</formula> with <formula id="166">$ \alpha_1 + \dots + \alpha_d \le n $</formula>, ordered with the exponent of the last coordinate varying slowest and that of the first coordinate fastest. For example, in 2D with <formula id="167">$ n = 2 $</formula>: <formula id="168">$ \boldsymbol{H} = [1,\ x_1,\ x_1^2,\ x_2,\ x_1 x_2,\ x_2^2]^T $</formula>; in 3D with <formula id="169">$ n = 1 $</formula>: <formula id="170">$ \boldsymbol{H} = [1,\ x_1,\ x_2,\ x_3]^T $</formula>. The first entry is always <formula id="171">$ 1 $</formula>.</para>
<para>The exponents <formula id="172">$ \boldsymbol{\alpha} $</formula> of every entry are listed once, at construction, as the factors <formula id="173">$ x_j^{\alpha_{kj}} $</formula> of nonzero exponent of each entry; an evaluation is then the product of the powers,  <formula id="174">$H_k = \prod_j x_j^{\alpha_{kj}} $</formula>, and its gradient that product with the factor of coordinate <formula id="95">$ i $</formula> replaced by its derivative,  <formula id="175">$ \partial H_k / \partial x_i = \alpha_{ki}\, x_i^{\alpha_{ki} - 1} \prod_{j \neq i}
x_j^{\alpha_{kj}} $</formula>. The powers are formed by multiplication, and the factors are multiplied in the order of the coordinates. Only the coordinates of nonzero exponent are factors of an entry: a factor <formula id="176">$ x_j^0 = 1 $</formula> would not change the product, and the derivative with respect to a coordinate that is not a factor is zero. Most exponents of a low-order basis are zero, e.g. all but three of the twelve of the linear basis in 3D. </para>
    </detaileddescription>
